# TODO - Autonomous AI quiz publication

## Objective

Finish admitted AI image delivery, autonomous publication, correction,
progress, cancellation, and recovery through the canonical operations.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when the teacher can ask an AI client for a quiz and the
registered product tools produce, publish, and verify it on Blooket without
requiring a routine editorial approval step. The flow must support admitted
question options and stable-ID media, show understandable progress/errors, and
correct supported mismatches without duplicate publication.

Actual attachment intake must work through an explicit, bounded path when media
is requested; do not assume ChatGPT attachments automatically arrive at MCP.
A genuine login/challenge/permission stop still requires the human. Show one
complete teacher-facing journey and focused checks; defer extra UX polish,
rare edge cases, and platform-only assurance to ongoing hardening.

## Implementation notes

Apply the two-field intake and normalization requirements from tasks 05-07 to
AI-assisted review. The AI supplies detected language, topics, and structured
normalized English metadata through admitted enrichment operations, preserving
the teacher's original name and description. Canonical asset paths remain
service-owned. Import, English source text, or an unsaved generated response is
not completed normalization.

Expose validated quiz list/read/create/edit, question type/seconds/media,
progress, and recovery through registered CLI commands and MCP tools. Keep local
JSON as automatic private drafts/execution state rather than requiring manual
file management or treating it as authority over fresh Blooket state.

Finish autonomous quiz publication, progress, cancellation, correction, and
recovery in the browser UI. Manual inspection remains available but is not a
pre-publication gate. Translate remaining states and actionable errors into
English and Spanish, preserving quiz and original-media language independently.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-14, a separate red-to-green UI regression rejected forged
final verification of a different revision, remote set ID, or operation count.
The presentation now requires the verified receipt to match its frozen
publication plan and retained remote identity before showing success.
An empty or replaced remote set ID also fails closed. The canonical service
still owns provider read-back, and no browser write was used in this test.

On 2026-10-14, a red-to-green UI regression demonstrated that a 133-step
admitted text quiz previously paused solely because the presentation layer
imposed a fixed 128-write limit. The orchestration loop now uses the same
10,000-operation structural limit as its validated progress envelope; the
canonical durable pacing and mutation budget still enforce account safety.
The corresponding reject-over-limit test performs no remote write. These are
synthetic progress tests, not provider acceptance of large question counts.

A restarted localhost service on 2026-10-14 served the new publication
module with HTTP 200 and JavaScript content type. In the owner's existing
Chrome for Testing profile, a newly opened background localhost test tab
loaded Quizzes with ten saved-draft cards and twenty controls, and no UI
error. Only the local read-only view was opened; the test tab was closed.

The connected browser reported zero pending jobs before and after the check.
All twenty-seven durable publication journal files retained their original
modification times, including seven unresolved attempt files. The rebuilt
Linux x64 development package also passed extracted-package verification.
These observations validate UI wiring and packaging, not remote publication.

On 2026-10-14, the Quizzes orchestrator was hardened for interrupted
publication across local draft edits. A resumed journal uses the immutable
snapshot revision reported by canonical status, not the latest saved draft
revision, so the service can enforce the original plan without authorizing a
new create. A malformed frozen revision or changed plan stops before the next
write; red-to-green tests cover both boundaries.

The UI also accepts zero-operation human and rate-limit stops before the first
snapshot, checks that progress belongs to the requested logical draft, and
refuses premature success. Safe application-owned failures are translated into
EN/ES instructions for sign-in, companion updates, remote conflicts, media,
lock contention and inaccessible journals; arbitrary error text is not shown.
These remain portable tests, not newly verified live Blooket publication.

On 2026-10-14, the local Quizzes view began exposing explicit publication
controls for saved text-only drafts through existing canonical commands.
One user intent checks durable status, advances only confirmed operations,
and performs a separate fresh verify after the last saved question.
Cancel stops the next operation and aborts the active local request.
An uncertain journal disables automatic progress and offers a separate
read-only reconciliation action; no status read triggers a remote write.

The browser module is served from the local service's explicit asset whitelist.
Portable regression tests cover orderly progress, cancellation, mismatched
plans, uncertain writes, and the rule that a step cannot claim verification.
Image/audio publication, editing existing quizzes, real autonomous AI client
acceptance, and the affected pending Chrome journals remain unresolved.

The browser runtime now has guarded text-only Create Set and Add Question
mechanics with exact post-submit read-back and persisted journal/budget
integration. Local drafts remain recovery/authoring state rather than a remote
Blooket draft authority.

## Blockers

Canonical external publication entrypoints and admitted media upload remain
functional work. No teacher approval is required before an otherwise admitted
publication; human intervention is reserved for genuine validation boundaries.
